From b14006628fb8756c1c27eee8d2a23fb4afdfa6b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 05:58:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Name the building, the flag and the inventory too

Two runs, and the owner correlation is unambiguous:

  run 1  serfs 15 and 41, owner=0  - the HOST advanced them, the peer did not
  run 2  serfs 35 and 40, owner=1  - the PEER advanced them, the host did not

Each machine dispatches its own player's serfs out of the castle and
leaves the other player's sitting idle_in_stock. That is not drift; it is
a gate on player identity somewhere in the simulation, and it points at
something the local machine does for the player the interface is holding.

Nothing in the simulation names the local player - scr_game, scr_player,
scr_building, scr_flag, scr_serf* and scr_inventory contain no reference
to net_local_player or to the interface - and the command routing does
not act locally before sending. So the gate is one level up from the
serfs, and with the widened parts run 2 says so: buildings and flags now
differ too, and the serfs are downstream of that.

So the same treatment for the other three collections. Per-slot digests
for buildings, flags and inventories travel with the check, and on a
desync the log names every differing one and dumps this machine's full
state for the first four of each: for a building its type, owner, pos,
progress, u, the five booleans and all three stock slots; for a flag its
paths, transporter, search fields and every occupied resource slot; for
an inventory its serfs-by-type table, resource counts and out queue,
which between them are exactly what call_out_serf reads.

net_log_serf_diff becomes net_log_obj_diff, taking the per-kind formatter
as an argument so all four report identically, and net_world_snapshot
gathers the parts and the four digest arrays in one place.

